Perfect. The easiest way to learn AWS is:

Don’t learn AWS from scratch. Translate what you already know from Azure.

You already understand this Azure flow:

Code
 ↓
Docker Build
 ↓
Docker Image
 ↓
Azure Container Registry (ACR)
 ↓
Azure Container Instance (ACI)
 ↓
Running FastAPI App

Now let’s convert it to AWS.

⸻

Azure vs AWS Mapping

Azure	AWS	Purpose
Azure Subscription	AWS Account	Billing & ownership
Azure Container Registry (ACR)	Amazon Elastic Container Registry	Store Docker images
Azure Container Instance (ACI)	Amazon Elastic Container Service with Fargate	Run containers
Azure Portal	AWS Management Console	UI
Azure CLI	AWS Command Line Interface	Command line access
Azure Key Vault	AWS Secrets Manager	Store secrets
Azure Blob Storage	Amazon Simple Storage Service	File storage
Azure PostgreSQL	Amazon Relational Database Service	Database


⸻

Your Exact Azure Deployment

You did:

Build Image
 ↓
Push to ACR
 ↓
Create Container Instance
 ↓
Run Application

AWS is almost identical:

Build Image
 ↓
Push to ECR
 ↓
Create ECS Task
 ↓
Run Application


⸻

Step 1: Build Docker Image

Azure:

docker build -t agent-test:latest .

AWS:

docker build -t agent-test:latest .

Exactly the same.

Why?

Because Docker is not Azure-specific or AWS-specific.

Docker is Docker.

⸻

Step 2: Test Locally

Azure:

docker run -p 6034:6034 agent-test:latest

AWS:

docker run -p 6034:6034 agent-test:latest

Again identical.

⸻

Step 3: Where Do Images Go?

In Azure:

ACR

stored images.

In AWS:

ECR

stores images.

Think:

ACR = Azure's image warehouse

ECR = AWS's image warehouse

Same job.

Different name.

⸻

Azure Registry Flow

You did:

az acr login

then:

docker push

to ACR.

⸻

AWS Registry Flow

You would do:

aws configure

This is AWS equivalent of:

az login

Then login to ECR:

aws ecr get-login-password

Then:

docker login

Then:

docker push

to ECR.

Same idea.

Different commands.

⸻

The Biggest Conceptual Difference

In Azure you used:

Azure Container Instance

which is extremely simple.

You basically clicked:

Image
 ↓
Run


⸻

AWS typically uses:

ECS + Fargate

instead.

This sounds scary but it’s not.

⸻

What Is ECS?

Think:

ACI = Azure service that runs containers

ECS = AWS service that runs containers

Very similar.

⸻

What Is Fargate?

Fargate is AWS saying:

“Don’t worry about servers. We’ll run the container.”

This is actually the closest AWS equivalent to Azure Container Instance.

Think:

ACI
≈
ECS + Fargate


⸻

Azure Container Instance Creation

You selected:

agent-test:latest

and clicked Create.

Azure then:

Pull Image
Create Container
Run Container


⸻

AWS Equivalent

You create:

ECS Task Definition

This tells AWS:

Which image?
How much RAM?
Which port?
Environment variables?

Example:

Image: agent-test:latest
Port: 6034
CPU: 1 vCPU
Memory: 2 GB

Think of it as:

Container Blueprint


⸻

Then AWS does:

Pull Image from ECR
 ↓
Start Container
 ↓
Give Endpoint

Exactly what ACI did.

⸻

What You Need to Learn First

If you are coming from Azure ACI, ignore:
	•	Kubernetes
	•	EKS
	•	EC2
	•	Auto Scaling
	•	Load Balancers

for now.

Just learn:

1. Docker

You already know this.

docker build
docker run


⸻

2. ECR

AWS version of ACR.

Store Images


⸻

3. ECS Fargate

AWS version of ACI.

Run Containers


⸻

Mental Model

What you already know:

Docker Image
      ↓
ACR
      ↓
ACI
      ↓
Running API

Translate it to AWS:

Docker Image
      ↓
ECR
      ↓
ECS Fargate
      ↓
Running API

If you fully understand the Azure deployment you described, you’re already about 70% of the way to understanding container deployment on AWS. The names change more than the concepts do.

The next thing I would explain is why AWS has both ECS, EKS, EC2, and Fargate, because that’s usually the next major confusion point for Azure developers moving to AWS.




