This is exactly where most people get overwhelmed in AWS.

In Azure, your mental model was simple:

ACR
 ↓
ACI
 ↓
Container Running

In AWS, suddenly people start talking about:

ECS
EKS
EC2
Fargate

and it sounds like four different ways to do the same thing.

The trick is:

They are NOT at the same level.

Some are container managers and some are machines that run containers.

⸻

First Understand The Layers

When you run a container, somebody has to provide a machine.

Think:

FastAPI
   ↓
Docker Container
   ↓
Machine (CPU/RAM)

The container cannot float in the air.

It needs a machine.

⸻

EC2 = Virtual Machine

Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud

This is simply a server.

Think:

Windows Laptop
or
Linux Server

in AWS.

Example:

4 CPU
16 GB RAM
Ubuntu Linux

AWS gives you a virtual computer.

⸻

What is your responsibility?

Everything.

Install Docker
Install Python
Configure Security
Patch OS
Monitor Server

You manage the machine.

⸻

ECS = Container Manager

Amazon Elastic Container Service

ECS is NOT a machine.

This is where many beginners get confused.

ECS is more like a supervisor.

Think:

Container Manager

It decides:

Which container?
How many copies?
Which machine?
Restart if crashed?


⸻

Fargate = AWS Manages The Machine

AWS Fargate

This is AWS saying:

Don’t worry about servers.

Think:

EC2
=
You manage machine

Fargate
=
AWS manages machine


⸻

ECS + EC2

Option 1:

ECS
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Containers

Meaning:

You provide servers
ECS manages containers

AWS asks:

How many EC2 servers?
Which size?
Which OS?

You answer.

⸻

ECS + Fargate

Option 2:

ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Containers

AWS asks only:

How much CPU?
How much RAM?
Which image?

AWS handles the servers.

This is the closest thing to your Azure Container Instance deployment.

⸻

EKS = Kubernetes

Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service

Now things get bigger.

Instead of AWS’s container manager (ECS), you use Kubernetes.

Think:

ECS
=
AWS Container Manager

EKS
=
Kubernetes Container Manager

Both manage containers.

Different technologies.

⸻

Real-World Analogy

Imagine a hotel.

EC2

Buy land
Build hotel
Hire staff
Run everything

Maximum control.

Maximum work.

⸻

ECS

Hotel Manager

Keeps track of guests (containers).

⸻

Fargate

Hotel building owned by AWS

You don’t manage the building.

⸻

EKS

A different hotel management company
(Kubernetes)

More powerful.

More complicated.

⸻

Mapping To Azure

This usually makes it click.

Azure	AWS
Azure Container Instance	ECS + Fargate
Azure Kubernetes Service	EKS
Azure Virtual Machine	EC2


⸻

Your Current Azure Deployment

You used:

ACR
 ↓
ACI
 ↓
Container

If AWS were chosen, I would recommend:

ECR
 ↓
ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Container

because it feels almost identical to ACI.

⸻

When Would You Use Each?

ECS + Fargate

Use when:

FastAPI APIs
AI Agents
Microservices
Internal Applications

Most likely where your Omnicore/Jarvis agents belong today.

⸻

ECS + EC2

Use when:

Need full server control
Custom software
Cost optimization


⸻

EKS

Use when:

Hundreds of services
Large engineering teams
Kubernetes standardization


⸻

EC2 Alone

Use when:

Legacy applications
Manual deployments
Custom environments


⸻

For someone with your current Azure Container Instance experience, the easiest mental translation is:

Azure Container Registry
        ↓
Azure Container Instance

becomes

Amazon ECR
        ↓
Amazon ECS + Fargate

Everything else (EC2 and EKS) is additional infrastructure knowledge that you can learn after you’re comfortable with the container flow.

